Version 1 of an order-event schema, and a backward-compatible version 2 that adds a field:

```json
// v1
{
  "type": "record",
  "name": "OrderEvent",
  "fields": [
    { "name": "order_id", "type": "string" },
    { "name": "amount", "type": "double" }
  ]
}

// v2 — adds discount_code with a default, so old data (which has no
// discount_code) still deserializes cleanly under the new schema
{
  "type": "record",
  "name": "OrderEvent",
  "fields": [
    { "name": "order_id", "type": "string" },
    { "name": "amount", "type": "double" },
    { "name": "discount_code", "type": ["null", "string"], "default": null }
  ]
}
```

A reader using v2 encountering a v1-written record fills `discount_code` with `null` automatically — no crash, no missing-field exception. That's backward compatibility, mechanically, not just as a policy statement.
